\description{
Defines the experimental design space for model evaluation. Takes a
\code{horizons_data} object with response data attached and specifies which
combinations of models, transformations, preprocessing, and feature selection
to benchmark.
}
\details{
\code{configure()} builds a configuration grid — the Cartesian product of all
specified modeling axes. Each row represents a distinct pipeline to
evaluate. This is the bridge between data preparation (\code{add_response()})
and model evaluation (\code{evaluate()}).

\strong{Outcome promotion:}

\code{add_response()} assigns \code{role = "response"} to joined variables.
\code{configure()} promotes exactly one response to \code{role = "outcome"} for
modeling. If only one response exists, it's auto-selected. Multiple
responses require explicit \code{outcome} specification.

\strong{Reconfiguration:}

Can be called multiple times on the same object. Previous configuration
is overwritten, any prior outcome role is reverted to "response", and
everything a previous \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} or \code{ensemble()} earned is
dropped: the \code{evaluation}, \code{models} and \code{ensemble} slots return to their
empty state, and the \code{horizons_eval}, \code{horizons_fit} or
\code{horizons_ensemble} class goes with them. All of it is keyed to the
outcome that is being replaced. The validation verdict is cleared too,
but the record of outliers \code{validate()} already removed is kept, since
those rows stay removed, and so is a \code{select_training()} record, which
describes the rows rather than the outcome. Because the record was not
drawn for the new outcome, \code{configure()} warns when a \code{select_training()}
record (other than \code{scope = "global"}) was drawn for properties that do not
include it (\code{horizons_warning_input}). Re-configuring an evaluated or
fitted object warns too (\code{horizons_warning_input}), naming what it
discards; re-configuring one that was only configured is reported in
verbose output and a note on the object's log. Either message says when
it cleared a validation, and with it a response-trim request, so that
\code{validate()} can be run again.
This enables the \code{purrr::map()} multi-outcome pattern:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{c("SOC", "POM_C", "pH") |>
  purrr::map(~base |> configure(outcome = .x) |> evaluate() |> fit())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Reproducibility:}

Every draw \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} and \code{ensemble()} make is seeded from the
run's seed and the draw's own name, so a run repeats from the seed it
records, whatever the order of the configurations, the workers or the
session's random number generator. That holds for \code{cubist} too because
each Cubist fit runs in a process of its own (\code{control_horizons(isolate = )}): its tuning searches \code{neighbors} from 0 to 9, and the neighbour
distance cutoff comes from a generator inside the Cubist C code that no
seed reaches and that carries on from every Cubist fit made before it in
the same process, which also keeps memory from each fit. With
\code{control_horizons(isolate = FALSE)} the fits run in the process that runs
them (the session, or a daemon on a parallel run), and a \code{cubist}
configuration's scores can then differ slightly between runs, and with
them, when candidates are close, the one chosen. See GitHub issue #51.

\strong{Choosing models for large spectral libraries:}

Cost scales very differently with predictor count (\code{p}) across
\code{MODEL_SPECS}. \code{rf} (ranger), \code{xgboost}, \code{lightgbm} and \code{elastic_net}
(glmnet) stay fast at full spectral resolution; \code{plsr} is built for
\verb{p >> n} and is not a concern either. \code{cubist} fits a linear model in
every rule, so its cost grows sharply with \code{p}: on the KSSL clay library
at 4 cm-1 (14,228 rows x 851 predictors, ~12.1M cells), not one of 25
tune tasks finished in 29.5 minutes, while the same config with
\code{feature_selection = "pca"} finished in 368 s (test RPD 3.82) — OSSL's
published pipeline is SNV -> PCA(120) -> Cubist (Safanelli et al. 2025,
PLOS ONE 20(1):e0296545). \code{validate()}'s P010 check reports when a
\code{cubist} config with \code{feature_selection = "none"} sits on a table whose
\code{n_rows * n_predictors} exceeds \code{CUBIST_MAX_CELLS} (a conservative floor
well under the measured failure point, not a benchmark); the fix is
\code{feature_selection = "pca"}. \code{svm_rbf} (kernlab) and \code{mars} (earth) also
scale with \code{p} on well-established grounds — an RBF kernel matrix costs
\verb{O(n^2 p)} to build, and MARS's forward pass searches every predictor at
every candidate knot — but neither has been measured at library scale,
and \code{validate()} does not report them. A slow run with either on a
full-resolution table is a signal to add \code{feature_selection} there too.
See GitHub issue #40.

\strong{plsr with \code{feature_selection = "pca"}:}

\code{plsr} scales its predictors to unit variance, and PCA's scores are
uncorrelated, so on them PLS re-projects what PCA has already projected:
its first component is already the least-squares fit on the kept
components (principal component regression at \code{pca_threshold}), every
count of \code{num_comp} is that same model, and the choice is one component.
The pairing runs, and tunes nothing; \code{evaluate()} says so once in its
tree and on the object's log. See GitHub issue #278.

\strong{Recipe settings:}

\code{sg_window} and \code{pca_threshold} are set once per object, not per
configuration: every configuration's recipe uses the same value, and
neither is part of the config id. \code{sg_window} is the Savitzky-Golay window
in grid points. It trims \code{(sg_window - 1) / 2} columns from each end of the
spectrum for every preprocessing method, \code{"raw"} and \code{"snv"} included, so
it matters even where no filter runs. The polynomial order is not a
setting: each method fixes its own (\code{"sg"} order 1; \code{"deriv1"} first
derivative, order 1; \code{"deriv2"} second derivative, order 3; the \code{"snv_"}
variants the same), and the cubic is why the window must be at least 5.
At order 1 the \code{"sg"} smoother is a moving average, so widening
\code{sg_window} widens a boxcar: it flattens peaks and shoulders narrower than
the window rather than preserving them, as a higher-order Savitzky-Golay
filter would.

Because the window counts grid points, its physical width depends on the
axis it runs on, and \code{standardize()} can still change that axis after
\code{configure()}. So the width is not stored here: \code{configure()} prints it in
cm-1 for the axis it sees, \code{evaluate()} records the width it actually ran
in \code{evaluation$recipe}, and \code{evaluate()} refuses a window as wide as the
spectrum. \code{pca_threshold} is the share of variance kept by
\code{feature_selection = "pca"} and is read by nothing else. Both defaults are
the values the recipe ran before they were settable. See GitHub issue #62.

\strong{Tuning ranges:}

\code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()}'s re-tune search each tuned parameter over a
range: its engine's, or horizons' where it sets one, or one given in
\code{tune_ranges}.
horizons sets two: Cubist's \code{committees} 1 to 25, since a fit's time grows
linearly with its committees and more of them seldom lowered the
cross-validated error by much, and plsr's \code{num_comp} 1 to 30. A range is
named by the parameter's id and applies to every configured model that
tunes it. The ids each model tunes:
\itemize{
\item \code{rf}: \code{mtry}, \code{min_n}
\item \code{cubist}: \code{committees}, \code{neighbors}, \code{max_rules}
\item \code{xgboost}: \code{tree_depth}, \code{learn_rate}, \code{mtry}, \code{min_n},
\code{loss_reduction}, \code{sample_size} (a proportion of the rows)
\item \code{plsr}: \code{num_comp}
\item \code{elastic_net}: \code{penalty}, \code{mixture}
\item \code{svm_rbf}: \code{cost}, \code{rbf_sigma}
\item \code{mlp}: \code{hidden_units}, \code{penalty}, \code{epochs}
\item \code{lightgbm}: \code{tree_depth}, \code{learn_rate}, \code{mtry}, \code{min_n}
\item \code{mars}: \code{prod_degree}, \code{num_terms}
}

A range is in dials' units: a parameter searched on a log scale takes its
range in log10 (log2 for \code{cost}), so \code{learn_rate = c(-3, -1)} searches
0.001 to 0.1. The data still cap \code{mtry} at the fewest predictors a
configuration's recipe keeps in any cross-validation fold or on the whole
training part, and \code{num_comp} at those and the smallest fold's rows less
one, narrowing a range given here, so no candidate asks a fold for more
predictors than it kept; a range whose lower bound is above that cap
fails the configuration, saying so. \code{configure()} refuses a name no configured model
tunes, a range that is not two finite numbers with the lower below the
upper, an \code{mtry}, \code{num_comp} or \code{hidden_units} range starting below 1 (each
counts the predictors, components or hidden units a fit uses, so 0 is no
model), and a fractional
bound on a whole-number parameter (typed where the model's engine is
installed, and again by \code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} once they have checked
the engines of the models they fit). An engine's own limits are not
checked: Cubist takes 1 to 100 committees and 0 to 9 neighbors, and a
range past them fails those candidates' fits, which the row reports. The
ranges are recorded as \code{config$tuning$ranges}, so \code{fit()}'s re-tune, when
it runs, searches the space \code{evaluate()} searched, and a checkpoint tuned
over other ranges is not resumed. \code{ensemble()}'s meta-learners keep their
own grids.

\code{mtry} has no range of horizons': rf, xgboost and lightgbm search it from
1 to the predictors the data cap it at, which on full spectra is about
1,700. A random forest's fit time grows with it: on one cross-validation
fold of full spectra, 500 trees took 148 s at an \code{mtry} of 41 and 3,051 s
at 1,200, on one thread. On the spectral data the package has been
benchmarked on, the values chosen on about 1,700 bands sat between 0.06
and 0.22 of them, and the cross-validated error changed little across
that span. So where time matters, \code{tune_ranges = list(mtry = c(1, 200))},
or a feature selection that keeps fewer bands, searches the faster part
of the range. That evidence is one data set, so the default searches the
whole range (#115).

mlp's networks are kept within a weight budget, \code{max_weights}. A network
of h hidden units on p predictors holds h(p + 2) + 1 weights, and nnet's
optimizer keeps a matrix of the weights squared, so a fit's memory grows
as about four bytes times the weights squared and its time the same way:
on 800 rows and 1,691 predictors, a fit of 5 units (8,466 weights) held
378 MB at 0.26 s an iteration, and one of 10 units (16,931 weights)
1.2 GB at 1.2 s. \code{hidden_units}' range is capped at the most units whose
network stays within the budget on the most predictors any of a
configuration's preps keeps (a cross-validation fold's or the whole
training part's), and when that narrows the range the configuration's
row and the verb's log say so with a note; nnet is given the budget as
its own limit (\code{MaxNWts}). At the default of 10,000 weights an mlp on
about 1,700 bands searches up to 5 units, and one on 250 selected bands
up to 39, so its default range of 1 to 10 is not narrowed there. Raise
the budget to search larger networks on wide predictors, at that cost,
or set it to \code{Inf} for none; a feature selection and
\code{tune_ranges = list(hidden_units = )} are the other levers. The budget is
recorded as \code{config$tuning$max_weights}, so \code{fit()} searches under the
budget \code{evaluate()} searched under, and a checkpoint tuned under another
is not resumed.

\strong{Outcome range:}

\code{outcome_range} is the physical range of the outcome, \code{c(lower, upper)},
either end possibly infinite. Every prediction the package scores or
serves is clamped to it: the test-set and out-of-fold predictions
\code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} score, the calibration predictions conformal
intervals are built from, \code{predict()}'s point predictions and interval
bounds, and the ensemble's. The default, \code{c(0, Inf)}, is the non-negative
floor the package applied before the range was settable, right for
concentrations, contents and most other soil properties. A signed property
such as δ13C or δ15N takes \code{c(-Inf, Inf)}, which clamps nothing, and a
bounded one such as a percentage may take \code{c(0, 100)}.

The range comes from what the property can physically be, not from the
data in hand. Setting it to the observed minimum and maximum clamps
predictions to the training range and, once test rows are in the table,
tunes the clamp on them. Prefer \code{-Inf} to an arbitrarily low finite floor:
the range's lower bound anchors the response bound \code{fit()} stores (see
\code{\link[=fit]{fit()}}), which allows half the span from that anchor above the training
maximum, so a distant floor loosens the guardrail. For outcomes between 10
and 20 the bound is 530 under \code{c(-1000, Inf)} and 25 under \code{c(-Inf, Inf)}.

The range is checked against the data: when an observed value of the
outcome lies outside it or is infinite, \code{configure()} aborts with class
\code{horizons_error_input}, and \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} and \code{ensemble()} repeat
the check before they fit anything. A range whose lower bound is negative
cannot be combined with the \code{"log"}, \code{"log10"} or \code{"sqrt"} transformations,
which are undefined for negative values and whose back-transforms floor
the predictions, and \code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} refuse to rank by \code{"rrmse"}
under it, since its denominator, the mean outcome, can be zero or negative.
One range applies to the one outcome an object models; to model several
outcomes with different ranges, give each \code{configure()} call its own. See
GitHub issue #76.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# Minimal (single response auto-selected)
hd |> configure()

# Explicit outcome with multiple responses
hd |> configure(outcome = "SOC")

# Full experimental design
hd |> configure(
  outcome         = "SOC",
  models          = c("rf", "cubist", "plsr", "xgboost"),
  transformations = c("none", "log"),
  preprocessing   = c("raw", "snv", "sg"),
  cv_folds        = 10L,
  grid_size       = 20L
)

# A wider Savitzky-Golay window and a tighter PCA for every config
hd |> configure(
  preprocessing     = c("deriv1", "snv_deriv2"),
  feature_selection = "pca",
  sg_window         = 15L,
  pca_threshold     = 0.99
)

# Cubist searched over up to 50 committees, and rf's mtry over 1 to 200
hd |> configure(
  models      = c("rf", "cubist"),
  tune_ranges = list(committees = c(1, 50), mtry = c(1, 200))
)

# A signed property: no floor at zero
hd |> configure(outcome = "d13C", outcome_range = c(-Inf, Inf))

# Multi-outcome pattern
c("SOC", "POM_C", "pH") |>
  purrr::map(~base |> configure(outcome = .x) |> evaluate() |> fit())
}

}
